The search page (`static/index.html`). It calls `GET /api/walk` only.

## 2. What does NOT exist

Do not invent these; the backend has no source for them:

- No `score`, `rank`, `pagerank` or any enrichment score: the app sorts by support, and the
  scores are not served to `reader`.
- No node `properties`, descriptions, addresses, coordinates, distances or URLs.
- No edge type, edge weight per row, edge id, or path (the list of nodes between the start and
  a row). A row says the hop it was reached at, not how.
- No person-level data: no patient, member or visit rows, counts below the privacy floor, or
  identifiers of care subjects.
- No search by label, no fuzzy match, no pagination, no depth or top-N parameter, $other_walks_text.
- No write endpoints. The connection is read-only.

## 3. Interpretation rules

- Rows are ordered by hop, then support (highest first), then node_id. Hop $depth is the
  farthest. A node appears once, at the first hop that reached it; the start node never appears.
- Each hop keeps at most $top_n nodes. A missing node is not proof of no link: it may have
  been cut by the per-hop limit.
- `support`: $support_meaning
- Privacy: $privacy_rule
- Edge types: each walk follows the edge types it names; a folded selection's support counts
  people, an unfolded one's counts edges; mixing is refused. A folded edge's weight is the
  number of distinct people (care subjects) behind it, never below the privacy floor; an
  unfolded edge has no weight (NULL) and no people behind it.
- Each served count is floored independently; differences between served counts are not protected.
- The data is as fresh as the last refresh of the graph version the served views point at.
  The app does not say which version that is.
